window.SPC_INDEX = [{"slug":"index","title":"Home","lede":"A twelve-lesson course for someone starting from zero. By the end you will be able to design a data pipeline, defend the choices you made, and name what you traded away to make them.","headings":[{"id":"who-this-is-for","text":"Who this is for"},{"id":"what-you-will-be-able-to-do","text":"What you will be able to do"},{"id":"the-path-through","text":"The path through"},{"id":"the-one-idea-to-leave-with","text":"The one idea to leave with"},{"id":"how-to-use-this-site","text":"How to use this site"}],"text":"Twelve lessons · start from zero How data gets from where it is made to where it is useful Twelve lessons, eighteen diagrams and a practice quiz, taking you from \"what is a data warehouse\" to designing a pipeline and walking someone through its failure modes. Start the course Jump to practice Who this is for Someone who has heard the words — pipeline, warehouse, Kafka, dbt, idempotent — and does not yet have a structure to hang them on. No prior data engineering experience is assumed. Basic SQL helps but is not required for the reading; it becomes necessary at the practice stage. The course teaches ideas rather than products. Tools are named so you recognise them in job descriptions and conversations, but the tools turn over every few years and the ideas do not. What you will be able to do Explain why analytics does not run on the application's database, in terms of how bytes sit on disk Choose an ingestion pattern for a source and defend it against the alternatives Recognise a pipeline that will silently produce wrong data, and say which specific failure will cause it Model a dataset into facts and dimensions with a clearly stated grain Take a vague request and turn it into a design with numbers, trade-offs and failure modes attached The path through Start here Foundations The reference pipeline, the OLTP/OLAP split, and the four constraints every design trades between. Everything later leans on this page. Building blocks Storage, ingestion, processing, orchestration Where data sits, how it gets there, how it is transformed, and what makes it all run on a schedule. The hard parts Reliability, quality, modelling, cost Idempotency, late data, schema changes, tests, star schemas and the bill. This is where junior and senior designs diverge. Designing The framework and worked examples An eight-step method for approaching any design, then two complete systems with the reasoning spelled out. Practice Twelve-week build path Projects that each end in something running on your own machine, from SQL to a streaming consumer you deliberately break. Test yourself Practice questions Scenario questions filterable by topic, with an explanation on every answer covering the tempting wrong one too. The one idea to leave with In backend engineering, the failure you fear is the system being down — loud, obvious, everyone knows within a minute. In data engineering, the failure you fear is the system being wrong — silent, still reporting success every morning, and capable of running for weeks while people make decisions on the output. Almost every practice in this course exists because of that difference. How to use this site Read in order the first time. Pages are sequenced by dependency, not importance, so later lessons assume earlier ones. Tick topics off as you go. Every section has a Done checkbox and the sidebar tracks the whole course. Search is fast. Press / anywhere to jump into the search box. Do the exercises. Several lessons end with something to try on your own machine. Those are where the learning actually happens — reading about idempotency is not the same as breaking a pipeline and watching the row count double."},{"slug":"foundations","title":"Foundations","lede":"This page assumes nothing and builds the model the rest of the course hangs off. If you only read one lesson, read this one — every later page refers back to it.","headings":[{"id":"the-problem-this-whole-field-exists-to-solve","text":"The problem this whole field exists to solve"},{"id":"how-this-differs-from-the-system-design-you-may-have-read-ab","text":"How this differs from the system design you may have read about"},{"id":"the-reference-pipeline","text":"The reference pipeline"},{"id":"oltp-and-olap-the-split-that-explains-everything","text":"OLTP and OLAP: the split that explains everything"},{"id":"why-the-storage-layout-is-the-whole-story","text":"Why the storage layout is the whole story"},{"id":"batch-and-streaming-are-a-spectrum-not-a-switch","text":"Batch and streaming are a spectrum, not a switch"},{"id":"the-four-constraints-you-are-always-trading-between","text":"The four constraints you are always trading between"},{"id":"what-a-good-data-system-looks-like","text":"What a good data system looks like"},{"id":"a-note-on-tools","text":"A note on tools"},{"id":"checklist","text":"Checklist"}],"text":"The problem this whole field exists to solve A company has an application. The application has a database, and that database is very good at one thing: answering questions about one customer, one order, one session, right now. Then someone asks: what did revenue do by region over the last three years, split by product category? You can write that query against the application's database. It will work — for a while. Then the company grows, the query starts taking four minutes, and one Tuesday it locks a table during a sale and the checkout page goes down. Now you have learned the founding lesson of data engineering the expensive way: the database that runs your business is the wrong place to analyse your business. So you copy the data somewhere else. And immediately you have a dozen new problems. How often do you copy it? What happens when the copy fails halfway? What if a row was deleted in the source? What if someone renames a column? Who notices when the numbers go wrong? Answering those questions well, at scale, for years, is data system design. A definition worth memorising Data system design is deciding how data moves from where it is produced to where it is useful — reliably, correctly, affordably, and in a way the next engineer can maintain. How this differs from the system design you may have read about If you have read about system design before, it was probably backend system design: design Twitter, design a URL shortener. That discipline optimises for many users doing tiny operations at once. Data system design optimises for few consumers doing enormous operations, usually not in real time. The questions are genuinely different. Backend system design asks Data system design asks How do I serve 50,000 requests per second? How do I process 500 GB a day without going bankrupt? How do I keep p99 latency under 100 ms? How fresh must this table be — 5 minutes or 6 hours? How do I avoid downtime? How do I recover when yesterday's data was wrong ? How do I shard this database? How do I lay files out so a query scans 1% of them instead of 100%? Notice the third row especially. In backend engineering, the failure you fear is the system being down — loud, obvious, everyone knows within a minute. In data engineering, the failure you fear is the system being wrong — silent, and capable of running for weeks while people make decisions on it. The reference pipeline Nearly every data system — a startup's first pipeline, a bank's platform, the thing your company already has — is a variation of the same five-stage shape. Learn it now, and for the rest of your career you can place any new tool or architecture by asking: which box does this fill? Data flows one way through five stages. Almost every tool you will ever hear about lives in exactly one of these boxes. Wrapped around all five stages are three concerns that apply everywhere and belong to no single box: Orchestration — what runs, in what order, when, and what happens when it fails. Observability — is it fresh, is it correct, what broke, what does it cost. Governance — who can see what, where the personal data is, how long it is kept. These three are the difference between a demo and a production system, and they are where most of the later pages in this course live. OLTP and OLAP: the split that explains everything This is the single most important idea in the course. Two kinds of database exist, built on opposite assumptions, and confusing them is the root cause of an enormous share of beginner mistakes. The same data, stored two different ways, because the two systems are asked completely different questions. OLTP (Online Transaction Processing) is the database behind your application. OLAP (Online Analytical Processing) is the database behind your analytics. Why the storage layout is the whole story Picture a table with 200 columns and one billion rows, and think about how the bytes sit on disk. Row storage keeps each record's fields next to each other: all of row 1, then all of row 2. Fetching one complete record is a single seek — perfect for \"show me order #4521\". But summing one column means reading all 200 columns of all one billion rows, because the values you want are scattered thinly across the whole file. Column storage keeps each column's values next to each other: every country , then every amount . Summing one column now reads one two-hundredth of the data. And because a column holds values of a single type — often with few distinct values, like country — it compresses five to twenty times better than mixed row data. Analogy A row store is a filing cabinet of paper forms: one folder per customer, every field about that customer inside it. Pulling one customer's file is instant. Answering \"what is the average age of all customers\" means opening every folder in the building. A column store is that same data typed into one long list of every name and a separate long list of every age. The average age is one list, read once. But reconstructing a single customer's full record now means visiting two hundred different lists. Where it breaks: a real column store does not visit those lists one at a time — it reads them in parallel, and a good one skips entire files using stored statistics. The analogy gets the trade-off right and the mechanics wrong. This is why a query that takes 40 minutes on Postgres takes 4 seconds on an analytics warehouse. It is not that the warehouse has faster disks. It is that it reads a hundredth of the bytes. The confusion to head off \"OLAP\" sounds like it should mean slow, offline, yesterday's data , and \"OLTP\" sounds like it should mean live and modern . Neither is true — both have \"online\" in the name and both serve interactive queries. The one-line test: OLTP is optimised for how fast one small request finishes; OLAP is optimised for how much data one big request can chew through. Freshness is a completely separate axis, and you choose it independently. Batch and streaming are a spectrum, not a switch The other axis you choose on is freshness: how old the data is when someone uses it. Beginners tend to treat this as a binary — batch or real-time — but it is a dial, and every notch to the right costs more money and much more engineering time. Freshness is a dial with a price attached. Streaming is roughly five to ten times the engineering cost of batch, and is needed far less often than people claim. The difference is not only cost. Batch work is bounded : you process yesterday's data, yesterday is over, so if the job fails you run it again and you are done. Streaming work is unbounded : there is no natural end, the job holds state between events, time and correctness become entangled, and \"just run it again\" stops being a sentence that means anything simple. The question to ask instead When someone asks for real-time, do not ask how fresh they want the data. Ask: what decision do you make with this, and how quickly do you make it? A monthly board report needs daily data. A marketing dashboard needs hourly. Blocking a fraudulent transaction needs milliseconds — and should not go through your warehouse at all. In practice, \"the executive wants real-time\" usually means \"yesterday's number feels stale\", which hourly solves at a fraction of the cost. The default is batch. Make streaming justify itself with a named decision that genuinely cannot wait. The four constraints you are always trading between No design maximises all four of these. Every choice in the rest of this course is a trade among them, and being able to name which one you traded away is most of what separates a considered design from a lucky one. 1 Correctness The numbers are right, and stay right when jobs fail, data arrives late, or someone reruns last month by accident. 2 Freshness How old the data is when it is used. The most expensive constraint to tighten, and the one most often over-specified. 3 Cost Compute, storage, and engineer hours. The third is usually the largest and never appears on the cloud bill. 4 Maintainability Can someone else understand, change and debug this in a year? Including you, having forgotten all of it. What a good data system looks like Five properties recur throughout this course. You will not fully understand them yet — each has its own lesson later. Read them now so the words are familiar when they arrive. Property Means Covered in Idempotent Running the same job twice gives the same result, not double the rows. Reliability Incremental It processes the new data, not all the data, every time. Modelling Reproducible Any output can be rebuilt from raw inputs, because raw was kept. Storage Observable You learn it is broken before your users tell you. Quality Loosely coupled A change upstream does not silently corrupt everything downstream. Quality A note on tools This course names real tools — Snowflake, Kafka, Airflow, Spark, dbt — so you recognise them in job descriptions and conversations. But tools turn over every few years and the ideas do not. Column storage was a good idea in 2005 and will be a good idea in 2035; the product implementing it will have changed names twice by then. When you meet a tool you have never heard of, ask two questions: which box of the reference pipeline does it fill, and which of the four constraints is it trading? That will get you further than reading its marketing page. Checklist Before moving on, you should be able to: Draw the five-stage reference pipeline from memory, and name the three cross-cutting concerns Explain why column storage makes an analytical query faster, in terms of bytes read — not just assert that it does Give the one-line test separating OLTP from OLAP without using the words \"online\" or \"analytical\" Explain to a colleague why their request for real-time data might be better served by hourly batch, and say what question you would ask them first Name the four constraints, and identify which one a design has sacrificed when you are shown one"},{"slug":"storage","title":"Storage","lede":"Where the data sits, what format it sits in, and how it is arranged. These three choices affect cost and speed more than almost anything else you will decide.","headings":[{"id":"the-shift-that-made-everything-else-possible","text":"The shift that made everything else possible"},{"id":"choosing-where-data-lives","text":"Choosing where data lives"},{"id":"what-a-lakehouse-is-without-the-marketing","text":"What a \"lakehouse\" is, without the marketing"},{"id":"file-formats-the-highest-return-decision-you-can-make","text":"File formats: the highest-return decision you can make"},{"id":"three-mechanisms-that-make-parquet-fast","text":"Three mechanisms that make Parquet fast"},{"id":"layout-how-you-arrange-files-decides-what-a-query-costs","text":"Layout: how you arrange files decides what a query costs"},{"id":"partitioning","text":"Partitioning"},{"id":"retention","text":"Retention"},{"id":"the-one-rule-about-raw-data","text":"The one rule about raw data"},{"id":"checklist","text":"Checklist"}],"text":"The shift that made everything else possible For decades, a database meant one thing: a machine with disks attached, where storage and the engine that read it were welded together. Needing more query power meant buying a bigger machine — and paying for its disks whether you used them or not. Storing five years of history meant paying for compute you were not running. Then cloud object storage arrived — Amazon S3, Google Cloud Storage, Azure Data Lake Storage — and broke the weld. Storage became a separate, absurdly cheap service, and compute became something you spin up, use, and switch off. This separation of storage and compute is the defining architectural change of the last fifteen years, and it is why you can now keep 500 TB of history and pay almost nothing until someone asks a question of it. What object storage actually is A giant key–value store where the keys look like file paths and the values are files. That is all. It is cheap (roughly $20–25 per TB per month), extraordinarily durable, and has no compute of its own. The trade-off: tens of milliseconds of latency per request, no transactions, no indexes. Everything in the rest of this page is, in one way or another, about adding those things back. Choosing where data lives Option What it is Use it when Watch out for Object storage Raw files in S3 / GCS / ADLS Landing zone for raw data, archives, anything huge and cold No schema enforcement, no transactions on its own Data warehouse Managed columnar SQL engine — Snowflake, BigQuery, Redshift Analytics and BI. The default for most companies Cost scales with data scanned; vendor lock-in Lakehouse ACID table layer over object storage — Iceberg, Delta Lake, Hudi Huge scale, multiple query engines, open formats Real operational work: compaction, maintenance, tuning OLTP database Postgres, MySQL Application state; small datasets served to apps Not for analytics at scale — see Foundations Key–value store DynamoDB, Redis, Cassandra Millisecond lookups by key: feature serving, caching No joins, no ad-hoc analysis Search index Elasticsearch, OpenSearch Full-text search, log exploration Expensive as a system of record What a \"lakehouse\" is, without the marketing You will hear the word constantly. Here is what it means mechanically. A lakehouse is four layers, and the interesting one is the table format — it is what turns a folder of files into something you can safely update. A folder of Parquet files in S3 is not a table. You cannot update a row, two writers will corrupt each other, and there is no way to ask what the data looked like last Tuesday. A table format — Iceberg, Delta Lake, Hudi — adds a metadata layer that tracks which files belong to the table at which point in time. That single addition buys you transactions, UPDATE and DELETE , schema evolution, time travel, and safe concurrent writers. What to actually choose as a beginner If your data is under roughly 10 TB and your team writes SQL, use a cloud warehouse. Full stop. The lakehouse path earns its complexity at large scale, with several processing engines, or when avoiding vendor lock-in is a real business requirement rather than a preference. Complexity has a running cost, and beginners consistently underestimate it. File formats: the highest-return decision you can make Format Layout Compression Schema Best for CSV row, text poor none Handing a file to a human. Avoid otherwise JSON / JSONL row, text poor implicit API payloads, semi-structured landing data Avro row, binary good embedded, evolvable Streaming records, Kafka, write-heavy work Parquet columnar , binary excellent embedded The default for analytics storage ORC columnar, binary excellent embedded Same niche as Parquet, common in Hive estates The same trade-off from Foundations, now as a storage decision: row formats serve records, column formats serve questions. Three mechanisms that make Parquet fast Worth knowing by name, because you will see all three in query plans and documentation: Column pruning — the reader fetches only the columns your query mentions. Selecting 3 columns from a 200-column table reads 1.5% of the file. Predicate pushdown — every file stores min/max statistics per column per row-group, so a filter like WHERE order_date = '2026-03-01' can skip entire files without decompressing them. Encoding and compression — dictionary and run-length encoding on same-type columns, then Snappy or ZSTD on top. The practical effect: a 10 GB CSV routinely becomes a 1–1.5 GB Parquet file, and queries against it run five to fifty times faster. Converting a raw CSV or JSON landing zone to Parquet is often the single highest-return change a beginner can make to an existing pipeline. The small file problem A streaming job that writes a file every 30 seconds produces 2,880 files a day and about a million a year. Every query must then open a million files, and the per-file overhead — list, open, read the footer — completely dominates the actual reading. Queries crawl; metadata operations start timing out. Target 128 MB to 1 GB per file. Below about 32 MB you are heading for trouble at scale. The fix is compaction : a scheduled job that rewrites many small files into fewer large ones. Table formats provide it as a built-in operation; on raw Parquet you must write and schedule it yourself. Budget for it as ongoing operational work, not a one-off. Layout: how you arrange files decides what a query costs In most cloud data platforms you are billed, directly or indirectly, for data scanned . So the cheapest query is the one that reads the least, and layout is how you make that happen. Partitioning Partitioning splits a table into physical subdirectories by the value of a column, almost always a date: s3://lake/orders/dt=2026-03-01/part-0001.parquet s3://lake/orders/dt=2026-03-02/part-0001.parquet A query filtered to March reads two directories instead of the whole table. That skipping is called partition pruning , and it is the workhorse optimisation of the entire field. Choosing a partition key well: Partition on what you filter by , which is a date in the overwhelming majority of cases. Aim for 100 MB – 1 GB per partition. This is the same size logic as file sizing, one level up. Never partition on a high-cardinality column. Partitioning by user_id across 10 million users creates 10 million tiny directories and will destroy performance rather than improve it. Do not over-partition by time. Hourly partitions on a table with 10 MB a day gives you 8,760 near-empty directories a year. Daily would be better; monthly might be better still. Partitioning versus clustering These get conflated constantly, and the distinction is simple once stated. Partitioning is physical separation into directories. It works for low-cardinality columns you filter on — dates, regions, event types. Clustering (also called sorting, or Z-ordering) physically orders rows within files so that min/max statistics become selective and files can be skipped. It is what you use for high-cardinality filter columns where partitioning would be a disaster — user_id , product_sku . One-line test: if the number of distinct values is in the thousands or more, you want clustering, not partitioning. Retention Nobody ever remembers to delete data, and storage bills grow forever. Decide at design time when partitions expire or move to cold storage. It is far easier to write the policy on day one than to negotiate it after someone notices the bill. The one rule about raw data Land what the source actually said, unmodified, and never edit it. This feels wasteful and is not. When you discover a parsing bug six months from now — and you will — the raw copy is the difference between reprocessing history in an afternoon and telling everyone the last six months of that metric are simply lost. It is also your only defence when someone claims the source system said something different from what you reported. Try this Take any CSV over a gigabyte. Load it into DuckDB, run an aggregation, and time it. Then COPY it to Parquet, run the identical query, and time that. Compare the file sizes too. Watching a 30-second query become a 0.4-second query on your own machine teaches this page better than any explanation can. Checklist Explain what separating storage from compute changed, and why it made keeping five years of history reasonable Say what a table format adds to a folder of Parquet files, and name three things it enables Choose between a warehouse and a lakehouse for a given team, and defend it on complexity rather than capability Name the three mechanisms that make Parquet fast, and explain predicate pushdown to someone who has never heard of it Diagnose the small file problem from a symptom, and say what fixes it Pick a partition key for a table given its query patterns — and say when to cluster instead"},{"slug":"ingestion","title":"Ingestion","lede":"Five patterns cover about 95% of real systems. Choosing the wrong one is recoverable; not knowing which traps come with the one you chose is not.","headings":[{"id":"the-awkward-truth-about-sources","text":"The awkward truth about sources"},{"id":"pattern-1-full-snapshot","text":"Pattern 1 — Full snapshot"},{"id":"pattern-2-incremental-with-a-high-water-mark","text":"Pattern 2 — Incremental with a high-water mark"},{"id":"pattern-3-change-data-capture","text":"Pattern 3 — Change Data Capture"},{"id":"pattern-4-event-streams","text":"Pattern 4 — Event streams"},{"id":"pattern-5-files-and-third-party-apis","text":"Pattern 5 — Files and third-party APIs"},{"id":"choosing-in-practice","text":"Choosing, in practice"},{"id":"checklist","text":"Checklist"}],"text":"The awkward truth about sources Every source belongs to someone else. Their schema is theirs, their uptime is theirs, and their decision to rename a column on a Tuesday afternoon is theirs. You are a guest in their system, and they very often do not know you exist. This is the root of most ingestion difficulty. The mechanics of copying data are easy. The hard part is copying it in a way that survives someone else changing their mind. Analogy Two ways to know what a neighbour is doing. You can look through their window every hour and note what changed — cheap to set up, but you miss anything that happens between looks, and you have no idea when they redecorate. Or they can agree to tell you whenever something changes — more to arrange, and far more reliable. The first is pull . The second is push . Every pattern below is one or the other. Where it breaks: a neighbour who tells you things is being polite; a source system that pushes events is making a commitment it can break silently. Push is more reliable, not automatically reliable. Pattern 1 — Full snapshot Copy the whole table, every run. Good Bad Trivial to build and reason about Does not scale — fine at 10 million rows, ruinous at 10 billion Self-correcting: yesterday's mistakes vanish today Heavy load on the source each run Handles deletes for free Loses history unless you snapshot to dated partitions Use it for small dimension tables, reference data, and any early-stage pipeline where simplicity is worth more than efficiency. Beginners often skip past this pattern as too naive. It is frequently the right answer, and it is always the right place to start. Pattern 2 — Incremental with a high-water mark Remember the largest updated_at you have seen, and next time ask only for rows above it. -- Cheap, common, and full of sharp edges SELECT * FROM orders WHERE updated_at > :last_watermark This is the most widely used pattern in the world and the one that quietly produces the most wrong data. Four failure modes, all of which you should know before writing one: The four ways a high-water mark lies to you Hard deletes are invisible. A deleted row is never \"greater than the watermark\", so your copy keeps a row that no longer exists in the source. Nothing errors. Your order count is simply too high, forever. updated_at does not always update. Bulk migrations and admin scripts routinely bypass it. If the column is set in application code rather than by the database, some code path is missing it right now. Open transactions skip rows. A transaction that began before your watermark but committed after it is silently missed — its rows have older timestamps than the mark you have already advanced past. Timezones. Mixing UTC and local time in a watermark is a classic silent-data-loss bug, and it goes unnoticed until a daylight-saving change makes it an hour worse. The standard mitigation for the third and fourth problems is an overlap window : re-pull the last few hours or days every run and deduplicate. That only works if your writes are idempotent — which is the subject of the Reliability lesson, and the reason it comes soon after this one. Pattern 3 — Change Data Capture Instead of querying the table, read the database's own replication log — the Postgres WAL, the MySQL binlog — and turn every insert, update and delete into an event. Tools: Debezium, Fivetran, AWS DMS. CDC solves exactly the failure modes a high-water-mark has, because it reads the record of what happened rather than inferring it from the current state. The catch is that CDC gives you a stream of changes , and you still have to apply them correctly. That normally means a MERGE keyed on the primary key and ordered by log position — get the ordering wrong and you will apply an old update after a newer one. Use it when the source table is large, deletes matter, or you need freshness under an hour. Do not reach for it on a 50,000-row settings table that changes twice a week; a snapshot is better. Pattern 4 — Event streams The application publishes events — order_placed , page_viewed — to a log such as Kafka, Kinesis or Pub/Sub. Consumers read from the log independently, each at its own pace. Four terms carry most of the meaning: Term What it means Why you care Topic A named stream of events The unit you subscribe to Partition A shard of a topic Ordering is guaranteed within a partition only — so key by user_id if one user's events must stay in order Offset A consumer's position in the log Replaying after a bug means resetting it Retention How long the log keeps data, often 7 days This is your replay budget when something breaks. It is a hard deadline on noticing Events and CDC look similar and are not Both produce a stream of records about changes, so they get lumped together. The difference is who decides what the record means. CDC describes rows changing : \"the status column of order 4521 went from pending to shipped \". You are reading someone's internal database structure, and you are coupled to it. Events describe things happening : \"order 4521 shipped, at this time, with this carrier\". The producer chose to publish that, and owns its meaning. CDC is easier to switch on without anyone's cooperation. Events are better data, because they survive the source team refactoring their schema. Pattern 5 — Files and third-party APIs A vendor drops CSVs on an SFTP server. A SaaS tool exposes a REST API. Unglamorous, ubiquitous, and where a surprising share of real data engineering time goes. Four things to get right: Pagination and rate limits — handle cursors properly, and respond to a 429 with exponential backoff plus jitter rather than an immediate retry. Retries — network calls fail. Assume it, and make retrying safe rather than merely possible. Deterministic landing paths — write to raw/stripe/charges/dt=2026-03-01/ so a rerun overwrites the same place instead of adding a second copy alongside the first. Keep the untouched response. Land the raw payload before you parse it. The single most valuable habit in ingestion Land raw, unmodified, before parsing. Always. Parsing logic has bugs. Vendors add fields. Six months from now you will discover that a field you ignored was important all along. If you kept the raw payload, that is an afternoon of reprocessing. If you did not, it is six months of missing data that no amount of engineering can recover. Choosing, in practice If the source is… Use Because A small, slow-changing table Full snapshot Simplicity beats efficiency; deletes handled free A large append-only table High-water-mark Rows never change, so the pattern's weaknesses do not apply A large table with updates and deletes CDC Only pattern that sees deletes and missed timestamp updates Genuinely event-shaped activity Event stream Decouples you from the producer's schema A vendor you do not control API or file drop You take what you are given — so land it raw One question decides more of this than any other: can rows change or be deleted after they are created? If no, high-water-mark is fine and cheap. If yes, you need CDC, or snapshots, or an explicit and documented acceptance that your copy will drift from the truth. Checklist Name the five ingestion patterns and give a source that suits each List the four ways a high-water-mark silently loses data, and say what an overlap window fixes and what it does not Explain what CDC reads, and why that makes deletes visible when a timestamp query cannot see them Explain the difference between a CDC record and a domain event to someone who thinks they are the same thing Say why partition keys matter in Kafka, in terms of ordering Justify landing raw data unmodified, using a concrete failure it prevents"},{"slug":"processing","title":"Processing","lede":"Raw data is almost never usable data. This lesson is about the machinery that turns one into the other, and the handful of terms that explain why it is sometimes slow.","headings":[{"id":"why-transformation-exists-at-all","text":"Why transformation exists at all"},{"id":"etl-and-elt-where-the-t-goes","text":"ETL and ELT: where the T goes"},{"id":"the-four-words-that-explain-distributed-performance","text":"The four words that explain distributed performance"},{"id":"the-sql-transformation-layer","text":"The SQL transformation layer"},{"id":"stream-processing-in-the-amount-you-need-now","text":"Stream processing, in the amount you need now"},{"id":"windows","text":"Windows"},{"id":"state","text":"State"},{"id":"watermarks","text":"Watermarks"},{"id":"full-refresh-before-incremental","text":"Full refresh before incremental"},{"id":"checklist","text":"Checklist"}],"text":"Why transformation exists at all Raw data arrives shaped for the system that produced it, not for the question you want to ask. Timestamps are strings. Money is in three currencies. The same customer appears as ACME Corp , Acme Corporation and acme corp. in three systems. Someone's \"order\" table has one row per shipment, and someone else's has one row per basket. Transformation is the work of turning that into something a person can query without a folklore of caveats. It is where most of the code in a data platform lives. ETL and ELT: where the T goes The classic acronym is ETL : Extract, Transform, Load. Take the data out, clean it in a separate engine, then load the clean result. This was the standard for decades, because warehouse compute was scarce and expensive and you did not want to waste it on cleaning. Then warehouse compute became cheap and elastic, and the order flipped. The letters are the same; the consequence is that in ELT your raw data survives, so a bug in transformation logic is recoverable without going back to the source. ELT won for four reasons, and it is worth being able to state them: Raw data is preserved , so you can re-derive anything when logic changes or a bug is found. Warehouse compute is elastic — you rent it for the minutes the transformation runs. SQL is accessible to far more people than Scala or Java, so analysts can own transformations. Logic lives in version control alongside tests, rather than inside a GUI tool nobody can diff. ETL is still right in three situations: heavy unstructured work (parsing PDFs, images, logs) that SQL cannot do; personal data that must be masked before it lands anywhere; and transformations needing a library your warehouse cannot run. The four words that explain distributed performance When one machine is not enough — Spark, or a warehouse's internal engine — work is split across many. You do not need to know how the scheduler works, but four terms explain most performance problems you will meet, and knowing them turns \"the job is slow\" into an actual diagnosis. Term What it is Symptom when it goes wrong Partition A chunk of data one task processes Too few: no parallelism, one core busy. Too many: scheduling overhead dominates Shuffle Moving data across the network so related rows meet on one node. Required by joins, GROUP BY and sorts The slow stage. Almost always the slow stage Skew One partition far larger than the rest 199 tasks finish in seconds, one runs for an hour Spill A task exceeds memory and writes to disk Sudden order-of-magnitude slowdown; usually caused by skew The classic skew story A job that ran in 8 minutes now takes 90. Nothing changed in the code. The cause is almost always a join key where one value dominates: a NULL user_id , or a \"guest checkout\" account that holds 40% of all orders. Every row with that key must land on one node, so one task does 40% of the work alone while the cluster idles. Adding machines does nothing — this is why \"just make the cluster bigger\" so often fails to help. The tell: look at task durations in the UI. Even distribution means a genuine scale problem; one outlier means skew. The SQL transformation layer Modern practice is to express transformations as version-controlled SQL models with declared dependencies. dbt is the dominant tool here, with SQLMesh as a notable alternative. The value is not the SQL — you could always write SQL. It is everything around it: Dependency resolution. You write ref('stg_orders') instead of a table name, and the tool works out the build order. Environments. The same code builds into a dev schema and a prod schema. Tests. Uniqueness, not-null and referential assertions run as part of the build. Documentation and lineage. Generated from the code, so it is accurate rather than aspirational. If you learn one transformation tool, learn this category. It is the closest thing the field has to a default. Stream processing, in the amount you need now If you process unbounded streams — Flink, Spark Structured Streaming, Kafka Streams — three concepts carry the weight. Windows You cannot aggregate an infinite stream, so you group by time. Window Shape Use for Tumbling Fixed, non-overlapping: 10:00–10:05, 10:05–10:10 Regular reporting buckets Sliding Fixed length, overlapping: every minute, covering 5 minutes Rolling averages, alerting thresholds Session Bounded by inactivity gaps User sessions, where the natural end is \"they stopped\" State The running data a job holds between events — counts so far, last value seen, an open session. State must be checkpointed to survive a restart, and it is the main source of operational pain in streaming. A batch job that dies has lost nothing; a stream job that dies has lost its memory of the world unless you arranged otherwise. Watermarks The engine's estimate of \"we have probably now seen everything up to time T\", which is what finally allows a window to close and emit a result. Anything arriving later is dropped or diverted, according to a policy you configure. This gets a full treatment in Reliability , because it is really a correctness topic wearing a performance costume. Why streaming is harder, in one sentence In batch, time is a column you filter on; in streaming, time is a property of the system itself — and the moment your correctness depends on the system's opinion of what time it is, everything gets harder. Full refresh before incremental Every transformation faces the same choice: rebuild the whole table, or process only what is new? Start with full refresh. It is simple, it is self-correcting, and it exercises every line of your logic on every run. Move to incremental when runtime or cost actually demands it, not in anticipation. The reason for that ordering is subtle and important: incremental logic is the code path that is not exercised on a normal run. Bugs there hide in the gap between \"what a full rebuild would produce\" and \"what a series of incremental runs produced\" — and that gap can grow silently for months. The patterns for doing it safely are in Modelling . Try this Take any transformation you have written and ask: if I ran a full rebuild right now, would the result match what is in the table? If you cannot answer confidently, you have found the most valuable test you could add. Checklist Explain why ELT replaced ETL, in terms of what became cheap — and name a case where ETL is still correct Define shuffle, and say why joins and GROUP BY cause one Diagnose skew from a description of task durations, and explain why adding nodes will not fix it Say what a transformation tool like dbt provides beyond the ability to run SQL Choose between tumbling, sliding and session windows for a given question Explain why you should reach for full refresh before incremental, in terms of which code paths get exercised"},{"slug":"orchestration","title":"Orchestration","lede":"What runs, in what order, when, and what happens when it fails. This is the layer that turns a collection of scripts into a system.","headings":[{"id":"why-cron-is-not-enough","text":"Why cron is not enough"},{"id":"the-dag","text":"The DAG"},{"id":"the-two-rules-that-matter-more-than-the-tool","text":"The two rules that matter more than the tool"},{"id":"rule-1-every-task-must-be-idempotent","text":"Rule 1 — Every task must be idempotent"},{"id":"rule-2-parameterise-by-logical-date-never-by-now","text":"Rule 2 — Parameterise by logical date, never by \"now\""},{"id":"backfills","text":"Backfills"},{"id":"failures-retries-and-alerts","text":"Failures, retries and alerts"},{"id":"how-to-structure-a-dag","text":"How to structure a DAG"},{"id":"checklist","text":"Checklist"}],"text":"Why cron is not enough The obvious way to run a pipeline daily is a cron entry. It works for exactly one script. Then you add a second that must run after the first, so you schedule it half an hour later and hope. Then the first job occasionally takes 45 minutes, and now the second one reads yesterday's data without complaining. Nobody notices for a week. That gap — between \"it ran\" and \"it ran after the thing it depends on succeeded\" — is the reason orchestrators exist. The DAG Work is modelled as a directed acyclic graph : tasks with declared dependencies, arrows one way, no loops. The orchestrator works out what can run in parallel, what must wait, and what to skip when something upstream fails. A realistic daily DAG. Note that testing is a task with the power to stop publication, not a report emailed afterwards. The common tools are Airflow (the incumbent, and the one most job listings name), with Dagster and Prefect as more modern, Python- and asset-first alternatives. Warehouse-native schedulers cover simpler cases. What an orchestrator gives you beyond a timer: Capability What it prevents Dependency management A task reading data its upstream has not written yet Retries with backoff A one-second network blip becoming a failed day Sensors and triggers Guessing at a schedule when you could wait for the file to actually land Backfills Hand-editing dates in a script forty times to rebuild a month Run history and logs Having no idea what happened on the 14th The two rules that matter more than the tool You can learn any orchestrator's syntax in a week. These two properties are what actually determine whether your pipeline is safe, and they hold regardless of which tool you pick. Rule 1 — Every task must be idempotent Running a task twice for the same logical period must produce the same result as running it once. This is what makes retries and backfills safe, and it is the property beginners most often violate without realising. -- NOT idempotent: the retry doubles every row INSERT INTO daily_sales SELECT ... FROM orders WHERE dt = '2026-03-01'; -- Idempotent: the retry replaces the same partition DELETE FROM daily_sales WHERE dt = '2026-03-01'; INSERT INTO daily_sales SELECT ... FROM orders WHERE dt = '2026-03-01'; -- Idempotent and atomic: MERGE on a stable business key MERGE INTO daily_sales t USING staged s ON t.dt = s.dt AND t.sku = s.sku WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET ... WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT ...; The full treatment — including what to do when there is no stable key — is in Reliability . For now, hold onto the test: if this task ran twice by accident, would anyone be able to tell? Rule 2 — Parameterise by logical date, never by \"now\" A task should receive the period it is processing as a parameter, and must never call CURRENT_DATE or now() to work out what to do. What happens when you break this rule It is June. You need to rebuild March because a bug has been found. You trigger the March 1st run. The task calls CURRENT_DATE , gets June 14th, reads June's data, and writes it into March's partition. Your history is now not merely still wrong — it is wrong in a new and much more confusing way, and the job reported success. Airflow calls the correct parameter the logical date or data interval ; every orchestrator has an equivalent. Use it in every query, every file path, and every API call. There are no exceptions worth making. Analogy A recipe that says \"use whatever is in the fridge\" produces a different meal every time it is followed. A recipe that lists its ingredients produces the same meal today, next week, or when someone else follows it. The logical date is the ingredient list. CURRENT_DATE is the fridge. Backfills A backfill is rerunning a pipeline over a past date range — because you fixed a bug, added a column, or a source restated its data. It is one of the most common activities in the job, which is why the two rules above matter so much: a backfill is just idempotency and logical dates being exercised at volume. Three practical points: Control the concurrency. Backfilling a year of daily partitions all at once will either exhaust your warehouse or cost a startling amount. Most orchestrators let you cap parallel runs; use it. Backfill into a separate location first when the change is risky, then swap. Rebuilding in place means a half-finished backfill is visible to users as half-wrong data. Watch out for the downstream. Rebuilding a table often means everything derived from it is now stale. Know what depends on what before you start — this is what lineage is for. Failures, retries and alerts Failures are normal. Design for them rather than treating each as a surprise. Failure Right response Transient — network blip, brief API 503 Automatic retry with exponential backoff. No human involved Upstream late — source has not delivered A sensor that waits, then alerts after a defined timeout. Do not silently process nothing Data wrong — a test failed Stop before publishing. A blocked pipeline is far cheaper than a published wrong number Logic broken — the code raised an error Alert a human. This is not retryable and retrying just delays the fix Success is not the same as correctness The most dangerous state in a data platform is a green dashboard. Every task succeeded, every run is a tick — and the source stopped sending rows four days ago, so your job has been faithfully processing an empty file each morning. Task status tells you your code ran. It says nothing about whether the data is right. You need both, and the second one is the subject of Quality . How to structure a DAG A few conventions that consistently pay off: One task, one responsibility. A task that extracts, transforms and loads cannot be retried partway, and its failure tells you nothing about which part broke. Make tasks small enough to rerun cheaply but large enough that the DAG is readable. A 300-task DAG nobody can look at is its own kind of failure. Prefer sensors to sleeping. \"Wait until the file exists\" is robust; \"start at 03:00 and hope\" is not. Keep business logic out of the orchestrator. Airflow should decide when things run; SQL and code should decide what they do. Mixing the two makes the logic untestable outside the scheduler. The exercise that teaches this page Schedule a pipeline daily. Let it run for a week. Then delete the last five days of output and backfill them. If your output afterwards is identical to what it was before, your pipeline is idempotent and correctly parameterised. If you get duplicate rows, or five copies of today's data, you have just found the bug this entire lesson exists to prevent — and you found it on your own laptop rather than in production. Checklist Explain what an orchestrator provides that cron does not, with a concrete failure cron allows Look at a SQL task and say whether it is idempotent, and fix it if it is not Explain the logical-date rule and describe exactly what goes wrong when a task calls CURRENT_DATE Plan a backfill of one month, naming the three things you would check before starting Say why a fully green DAG is not evidence that the data is correct"},{"slug":"reliability","title":"Reliability","lede":"Everything up to here describes systems that work. This lesson is about what makes them keep working — and it is where junior and senior designs visibly diverge.","headings":[{"id":"assume-every-step-fails-halfway","text":"Assume every step fails halfway"},{"id":"the-three-delivery-guarantees","text":"The three delivery guarantees"},{"id":"three-ways-to-make-a-write-idempotent","text":"Three ways to make a write idempotent"},{"id":"1-overwrite-by-partition","text":"1. Overwrite by partition"},{"id":"2-merge-on-a-stable-key","text":"2. Merge on a stable key"},{"id":"3-deduplicate-on-read-or-in-staging","text":"3. Deduplicate on read or in staging"},{"id":"late-and-out-of-order-data","text":"Late and out-of-order data"},{"id":"handling-it-in-batch-the-lookback-window","text":"Handling it in batch: the lookback window"},{"id":"handling-it-in-streaming-watermarks","text":"Handling it in streaming: watermarks"},{"id":"restatement-deciding-what-history-means","text":"Restatement: deciding what history means"},{"id":"checklist","text":"Checklist"}],"text":"Assume every step fails halfway A job writes 60% of its output and the machine dies. A network call succeeds but the acknowledgement is lost, so the sender retries something that already happened. A source restates last week's numbers without telling anyone. None of these are edge cases. At any real volume they happen weekly. The question is not whether your pipeline will be interrupted mid-write — it will — but what state it leaves behind when it is. The three delivery guarantees Real systems give you at-least-once. Exactly-once is not something you receive — it is something you build, by making the write itself safe to repeat. The reasoning is worth walking through, because it explains why \"exactly-once\" is such a slippery marketing term. A sender transmits a record and waits for an acknowledgement. The acknowledgement does not arrive. Two possibilities: the record never landed, or it landed and the acknowledgement was lost. The sender cannot tell these apart. It must either retry — risking a duplicate — or not — risking loss. There is no third option available at the transport layer, however sophisticated. So real systems choose at-least-once and accept duplicates. You then make the duplicates harmless by ensuring that writing the same record twice has the same effect as writing it once. That is idempotency, and it is why practitioners often say \"effectively-once\" instead. \"Exactly-once\" on a product page When a tool advertises exactly-once, it almost always means exactly-once within its own boundaries — its internal state and its own sink, using transactions it controls end to end. The moment your pipeline writes to something that tool does not control — an external API, a table another job also writes to, a file in a bucket — the guarantee ends at that boundary. It is a real feature; it is just narrower than the name suggests. Read it as \"we handle our part\", and handle yours. Three ways to make a write idempotent In rough order of preference. 1. Overwrite by partition Replace the entire logical partition you are processing, rather than adding to it. DELETE FROM daily_sales WHERE dt = '2026-03-01'; INSERT INTO daily_sales SELECT ... WHERE dt = '2026-03-01'; Simple, robust, and requires no unique key at all. It is a large part of why date partitioning is so universal — partitioning is not only a performance decision, it is a correctness one. 2. Merge on a stable key MERGE INTO orders t USING staged s ON t.order_id = s.order_id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET ... WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT ...; Handles updates as well as duplicates, which overwrite-by-partition does not when a record moves between partitions. It requires a genuine business key — order_id , not a row number. 3. Deduplicate on read or in staging SELECT * FROM ( SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER ( PARTITION BY event_id ORDER BY ingested_at DESC) AS rn FROM raw_events ) WHERE rn = 1 Keeps every copy and picks a winner. Useful when raw must stay append-only, at the cost of doing the work on every read. Ask for a unique key on day one All three techniques depend on being able to identify the same record twice. Ask every source for a stable unique identifier at the start of the project — retrofitting one is painful and sometimes impossible. If a source genuinely has none, synthesise a deterministic hash of the fields that define uniqueness. Never a random UUID generated at ingestion time: it differs on every rerun, which defeats the entire purpose while looking like it solved the problem. Late and out-of-order data Two clocks exist, and conflating them produces numbers that are wrong in ways nobody notices. Event time — when the thing actually happened. Processing time — when your system saw it. Monday's total was written on Tuesday and was wrong from the moment it was written. Nothing failed, nothing alerted, and the number looks perfectly reasonable. This is not an exotic scenario. Mobile apps buffer events offline. Vendors restate attribution data for three days. Payment providers attach refunds to charges from weeks ago. Any system with humans or devices at the edge has late data, always. Handling it in batch: the lookback window Do not reprocess only yesterday. Reprocess the last N days on every run, where N comes from the observed lateness distribution rather than a guess. Measure it: for a sample of records, compute ingested_at - event_time and look at the p99 and p99.9. If 99.9% of events arrive within four days, a seven-day lookback covers you with margin. Costs a little compute every night; prevents a class of silently wrong numbers. This is only safe if the write is idempotent — reprocessing seven days with an INSERT would give you seven copies. The two ideas on this page depend on each other, which is why they share a lesson. Handling it in streaming: watermarks A watermark is the engine's assertion that everything up to time T has probably arrived, which lets a window close and emit a result. Anything later is dropped, or routed to a side output for separate handling. The allowed lateness is a configuration value, and it is genuinely a business decision, not a technical default. \"We will count events up to 2 hours late and discard the rest\" is a statement about what the numbers mean, and someone other than you should agree to it. Store both timestamps. Always. Every record should carry event_time (when it happened) and ingested_at (when you received it). It costs two columns. Aggregate on event time, monitor freshness on ingestion time, and measure lateness by subtracting one from the other. Without both, \"why is last Tuesday's number different now?\" is unanswerable. Restatement: deciding what history means If you reprocess late data, historical numbers change. Someone will notice, and the conversation is much better if you had it in advance. Policy Means Suits Always current Numbers update whenever new data arrives, silently Product analytics, experimentation Announced restatement History can change, but a change is communicated Marketing attribution, operational reporting Frozen after a period After close of month, the number never moves again Finance, regulatory reporting Finance teams care about this intensely. Discovering their expectations after you have silently changed last quarter's revenue is a genuinely bad day, and one that damages trust in the platform for a long time afterwards. The exercise Run a streaming consumer that writes to a table. Kill it mid-run. Restart it. Count the rows. Then add an event_id and a deduplicating read, and do it again. The difference between the two row counts is the whole of this lesson, and seeing it on your own machine makes it permanent in a way reading cannot. Checklist Explain why exactly-once delivery is not achievable at the transport layer, using the lost-acknowledgement argument Make a non-idempotent write idempotent, choosing correctly between overwrite, merge and dedup for a given situation Say why a random UUID assigned at ingestion is a broken deduplication key Distinguish event time from processing time, and give a real scenario where the gap is days Choose a lookback window from a lateness distribution rather than by intuition Name the three restatement policies and say which one a finance team will want"},{"slug":"quality","title":"Quality","lede":"Broken pipelines page you. Wrong data does not — which is exactly what makes it the more dangerous failure, and the one worth building defences against.","headings":[{"id":"the-failure-that-does-not-announce-itself","text":"The failure that does not announce itself"},{"id":"schema-changes-which-ones-are-safe","text":"Schema changes: which ones are safe"},{"id":"defences-from-cheap-to-serious","text":"Defences, from cheap to serious"},{"id":"data-contracts","text":"Data contracts"},{"id":"the-six-test-categories","text":"The six test categories"},{"id":"where-tests-belong-and-what-failure-should-do","text":"Where tests belong, and what failure should do"},{"id":"observability","text":"Observability"},{"id":"a-useful-habit-reconciliation","text":"A useful habit: reconciliation"},{"id":"checklist","text":"Checklist"}],"text":"The failure that does not announce itself Imagine a pipeline that has run successfully every morning for five weeks. Green ticks the whole way down. It has also, for those five weeks, been undercounting revenue by 4% because an upstream team added a new order status your CASE statement does not handle. Nobody knows. Decisions have been made on those numbers. When it is finally discovered, the cost is not the bug — it is the five weeks, and the fact that for the next year people will ask \"are we sure this number is right?\" before every meeting. That is the failure this lesson defends against. It is a different problem from uptime, and it needs different tools. Schema changes: which ones are safe Schemas change. The question is whether a given change is compatible with readers that already exist. Change Safe? Why Add a nullable column Safe Existing readers ignore it Add a required column Breaking Old rows have no value for it Drop a column Breaking Downstream queries referencing it fail Rename a column Breaking It is a drop and an add wearing a disguise Widen a type (int → bigint) Usually safe Every old value still fits Narrow a type (bigint → int) Breaking Silent overflow or truncation Change the meaning, not the type Worst of all amount switches from dollars to cents. Nothing errors. Every number is wrong by 100× Why the last row is the worst Every other change in that table produces an error somewhere, and an error is a gift — it tells you immediately, in a log, with a line number. A semantic change produces no error at all. The pipeline runs, the types match, the tests pass, and the numbers are wrong. This is why schema validation alone is insufficient and why the distribution tests below matter: they are the only mechanism that catches a value which is well-formed and meaningless. Defences, from cheap to serious Never SELECT * into a persisted model. Pin the columns you depend on, so a new upstream column cannot surprise you and a dropped one fails loudly at build time. Land raw permissively, enforce at the first transformation. If raw is JSON, an unexpected field cannot break ingestion. Apply the schema one step later, where failing is safe. Use a schema registry (common with Avro on Kafka) to reject incompatible producer changes at publish time — before the bad data exists. Write a data contract. See below. Data contracts A data contract is an agreement between the team producing data and the teams consuming it, covering schema, semantics, freshness, and how changes are made. It sounds bureaucratic. In practice the lightweight version is a schema file living in the producer's repository plus a CI check that fails their build if they break it — and that single arrangement prevents most cross-team data incidents, because it moves the failure from your 3 a.m. pager to their pull request. Analogy An API has a version, documentation, a deprecation policy, and a test suite that fails when someone breaks it. Nobody would ship a public API by letting any developer rename a field whenever they liked. A data contract is that same discipline applied to a table or a topic. The reason data usually lacks it is historical, not principled: analytics started as people reading each other's databases, and the habit stuck. Where it breaks: an API has one obvious owner and a visible caller list. A table often has neither, which is why contracts need lineage to be enforceable — you must be able to see who would break. The six test categories Treat these as a checklist to apply to every table that matters. Most real data incidents are caught by one of these six, and teams that write tests ad hoc reliably miss two or three of them. # Test Asks Catches 1 Freshness Is max(updated_at) within the SLA? A source that silently stopped delivering 2 Volume Is today's row count in the expected range? Partial loads, double loads, a filter that went wrong 3 Uniqueness Is the primary key actually unique? Broken joins, duplicate writes, fan-out 4 Not-null Are required fields populated? Upstream schema drift, failed lookups 5 Referential Do all foreign keys resolve? Late-arriving dimensions, orphaned facts 6 Distribution Is revenue ≥ 0? Is status='unknown' under 1%? Semantic changes — the ones nothing else catches Uniqueness deserves special attention A duplicate in a dimension table does not produce an error. It produces a join that returns two rows where it should return one — so every measure joined through it silently doubles. This is the most common cause of \"the dashboard says we made twice as much as we did\", and a single uniqueness test on the dimension's key would have caught it at build time. If you only ever write one test per table, write this one. Where tests belong, and what failure should do Tests on raw data catch other people's problems; tests on marts catch your own. You need both, and they ask different questions. Each test also needs a decision about severity: Blocking — the pipeline stops and nothing downstream is published. Correct for correctness-critical tables, where wrong data is worse than late data. This is sometimes called a circuit breaker. Warning — the pipeline continues, someone is notified. Correct for noisy heuristics and soft thresholds. Do not make everything blocking The instinct is to block on every test, since wrong data is bad. The result is a pipeline that halts three times a week on soft thresholds, an alert channel everyone mutes, and eventually a real failure that nobody looks at. An ignored alert is worse than no alert, because it creates the appearance of monitoring without the substance. Block on the small number of tests where publishing wrong data is genuinely worse than publishing nothing. Observability You should find out a pipeline is broken from your monitoring, not from an executive's email. Four things to build: 1 Freshness SLAs Publish, per important table, when it will be updated by. \"Orders mart is fresh as of 06:00 daily\" is a contract — monitor it and alert on breach. 2 Lineage A graph of which tables feed which. Answers \"what breaks if I change this?\" and \"where did this bad number come from?\" Modern transformation tools generate it from your code. 3 Run metadata Log durations, rows in, rows out, and cost per run — then trend them. A job whose runtime has quietly tripled over a month is a future 3 a.m. incident announcing itself early. 4 Runbooks For each critical pipeline: how to rerun it, what is safe to backfill, who owns the source, who to tell. Written before you need it, because you will need it at the worst possible moment. A useful habit: reconciliation The strongest test you can write compares your number to an independently produced number for the same thing. If Stripe says you processed $482,110 yesterday and your warehouse says $479,884, something is wrong, and you know it within a day rather than at quarter-end. Reconciliation tests catch entire categories of error that no schema check or null test could — missing rows, double-counted joins, currency mistakes, silently dropped partitions — because they check the meaning rather than the shape. Wherever a source system publishes its own totals, compare against them. Checklist Classify a proposed schema change as safe or breaking, and explain why a semantic change is more dangerous than a dropped column Describe the lightweight version of a data contract and where it lives Name all six test categories from memory and say what each one catches Explain why a duplicate in a dimension table doubles your revenue figures Decide whether a given test should block or warn, and argue against blocking on everything Design a reconciliation test for a pipeline you have built"},{"slug":"modeling","title":"Modelling","lede":"Storage decides what a query costs. Modelling decides whether anyone can write the query at all — and whether two people asking the same question get the same answer.","headings":[{"id":"the-problem-modelling-solves","text":"The problem modelling solves"},{"id":"layers","text":"Layers"},{"id":"dimensional-modelling","text":"Dimensional modelling"},{"id":"a-note-on-normalisation","text":"A note on normalisation"},{"id":"slowly-changing-dimensions","text":"Slowly changing dimensions"},{"id":"incremental-patterns","text":"Incremental patterns"},{"id":"lambda-and-kappa-briefly","text":"Lambda and Kappa, briefly"},{"id":"checklist","text":"Checklist"}],"text":"The problem modelling solves A company has 400 tables copied from six systems. An analyst wants monthly revenue by customer segment. To get it they must know which of the three orders tables is authoritative, that cancelled orders have status = 9 , that the EU subsidiary reports in euros, and that test accounts must be excluded using a rule someone explained in Slack in 2024. They work it out and build a dashboard. Someone else does the same thing next month and gets a different number, because they excluded test accounts differently. Modelling is the discipline of doing that work once , in one place, so the answer is consistent and the knowledge is in code rather than in people's heads. Layers Every mature platform organises tables into layers. The names differ — bronze/silver/gold, raw/staging/marts, landing/base/reporting — and the idea is identical. Each layer reads only from the one above it. That single restriction is what stops business logic from being reinvented in six places. Three rules make this work: Raw is sacred. Never transform it in place. It is your ability to rebuild everything, and your evidence of what the source actually said. Each layer reads only from the layer above. No mart querying raw directly — that is how logic gets duplicated and then diverges. Business logic lives in one layer. If \"active customer\" is defined in four marts, you will get four numbers in four dashboards, and a meeting about which one is right that produces no useful outcome. Dimensional modelling The dominant way to model analytics data, from Ralph Kimball. Two kinds of table: Facts — things that happened. Long and narrow: mostly numbers and foreign keys. fct_orders , fct_page_views . Dimensions — the context around those things. Short and wide: mostly descriptive text. dim_customer , dim_product , dim_date . The star shape is the point: every dimension is one join from the fact, so any combination of filters is a simple, fast query. It has survived thirty years of fashion for two reasons. It is fast, because the join graph is one hop deep. And non-engineers can understand it — an analyst can look at a star schema and work out how to answer their question without asking you, which is worth more than any performance benefit. Grain is the concept to get right The grain is what exactly one row of a fact table represents. Not \"orders\" — \"one row per order line item\" . Declare it explicitly, in writing, before you write any SQL. Almost every dimensional modelling disaster traces back to a fact table with an ambiguous or mixed grain. If some rows are orders and some are line items, then summing revenue double-counts, and the error is invisible until someone reconciles against finance. The test: can you state the grain in one sentence beginning \"one row per…\"? If not, the table is not ready to be built. A note on normalisation OLTP databases normalise — third normal form, no duplicated values — to avoid update anomalies, because they are written to constantly. Analytics tables are written once and read thousands of times, so the trade-off inverts: analytics deliberately denormalises. Storage is cheap, joins are expensive, and an analyst reading country_name directly rather than joining three tables to find it is a genuine productivity gain. If you come from application development, this will feel wrong for about a month. Slowly changing dimensions A customer moves from Berlin to Munich. Last year's orders — were they placed by a Berlin customer or a Munich one? Both answers are defensible, and you must choose deliberately. Type What it does Result Use for Type 1 Overwrite the old value Only the current truth exists; history is rewritten Correcting typos, fields where history is meaningless Type 2 Add a new row with validity dates Both versions exist; facts join to the version that was current at the time Anything where historical reporting must reflect the world as it was A Type 2 dimension looks like this: customer_key customer_id city valid_from valid_to is_current 101 C-77 Berlin 2024-01-01 2025-06-30 false 102 C-77 Munich 2025-07-01 9999-12-31 true Note the two different keys. customer_id is the business key — the real customer. customer_key is a surrogate key identifying this version of them. Facts join on the surrogate key, which is what makes a 2024 order correctly attribute to Berlin. This is what people mean by point-in-time correct , and it matters enormously for both historical reporting and machine learning training data. Type 2 costs complexity, so apply it only where history genuinely matters. Ask the business rather than guessing — and be aware that \"do you need to see what this looked like last year?\" gets a more accurate answer than \"do you want Type 2?\". Incremental patterns How you update a table when new data arrives. The choice interacts directly with idempotency from Reliability . Pattern How Use when Idempotent? Full refresh Rebuild the whole table Small tables, complex logic, correctness over cost Yes, inherently Append Insert new rows only Immutable events that never change No — needs a dedup key Insert-overwrite Replace whole partitions Records within a period can change Yes Merge / upsert Match on key, update or insert Mutable records with a reliable key (CDC output) Yes Keep the ability to rebuild Whatever incremental pattern you adopt, make sure a full rebuild is still possible and occasionally tested. Incremental logic drifts — a bug shipped three months ago leaves a permanent dent in history that only a rebuild will remove. Some teams schedule a full rebuild monthly for exactly this reason. It costs one expensive night and buys certainty that incremental output still matches what the logic says it should be. Lambda and Kappa, briefly Two named architectures you will hear about, worth recognising: Lambda — run a batch layer (accurate, slow) and a speed layer (fast, approximate) in parallel and merge them at query time. Its flaw is famous: two implementations of the same business logic , which inevitably drift apart. Kappa — one streaming pipeline handles everything; reprocessing means replaying the log from the start. One codebase, but it demands long log retention and real streaming maturity. Most teams land somewhere pragmatic: batch for the bulk of analytics, one or two streaming paths where the business case is clear. Knowing the names and Lambda's dual-implementation flaw is enough — and note the nuance in Worked examples , where a Lambda-shaped design is acceptable precisely because the two paths compute different things and so cannot drift. Checklist Explain what each layer does and why marts must not read from raw Sort a list of tables into facts and dimensions, and justify each State the grain of a fact table in one sentence, and explain what goes wrong when it is mixed Explain why analytics denormalises when application databases normalise Choose between SCD Type 1 and Type 2 for a given field, based on a question you would ask the business Explain what a surrogate key does in a Type 2 dimension that a business key cannot Pick an incremental pattern for a table and say whether it is idempotent as written"},{"slug":"cost","title":"Cost & governance","lede":"Two topics that beginners treat as someone else's job, and that senior engineers treat as design inputs. Both are much cheaper to get right early than to retrofit.","headings":[{"id":"cost-is-a-design-constraint-not-an-afterthought","text":"Cost is a design constraint, not an afterthought"},{"id":"where-the-money-actually-goes","text":"Where the money actually goes"},{"id":"the-cost-nobody-puts-on-the-invoice","text":"The cost nobody puts on the invoice"},{"id":"governance-design-inputs-not-paperwork","text":"Governance: design inputs, not paperwork"},{"id":"know-where-the-personal-data-is","text":"Know where the personal data is"},{"id":"minimise","text":"Minimise"},{"id":"mask-or-tokenise-early","text":"Mask or tokenise early"},{"id":"access-control","text":"Access control"},{"id":"deletion-and-retention","text":"Deletion and retention"},{"id":"audit","text":"Audit"},{"id":"putting-a-number-on-a-design","text":"Putting a number on a design"},{"id":"checklist","text":"Checklist"}],"text":"Cost is a design constraint, not an afterthought In a cloud data platform, cost is dominated by compute on data scanned — not by storage. Storage is genuinely cheap, at roughly $20–25 per TB per month. Reading that storage, repeatedly, all day, is where the bill comes from. That single fact reframes most optimisation work. You are not trying to make queries faster in the abstract; you are trying to make them read less . Every technique below is a version of that idea. Analogy Renting a warehouse is cheap. Sending a truck to it is not. If you send a truck to fetch one box, you pay for the truck; if you send forty trucks a day because nothing is labelled and each one has to search the whole building, you pay forty times over — and the fix is labelling, not a bigger truck. Partitioning is the labelling. Bigger compute is the bigger truck. Beginners reliably reach for the truck. Where the money actually goes Cause Symptom Fix Missing partition filters Every query scans the full table Partition properly; require a date filter on huge tables Reprocessing all history each run Nightly job cost grows linearly with age of the company Incremental models A dashboard auto-refreshing every 60 seconds One dashboard outspends the entire pipeline Cache, materialise the aggregate, refresh on a sane cadence SELECT * on a 300-column table Reading 300 columns to use 4 Name the columns — columnar storage rewards this enormously Idle always-on clusters Steady spend overnight and at weekends Auto-suspend, serverless, or right-size the warehouse Streaming where batch would do Always-on compute for data nobody reads until morning Revisit the freshness requirement with the actual consumer Small files Query time dominated by opening files Compaction — see Storage The dashboard that costs more than the pipeline This one surprises people every time. A BI dashboard set to auto-refresh every minute, left open on ten screens across the office, issues 14,400 queries a day. If each scans 50 GB, that is 720 TB scanned daily to display numbers that change once an hour. The pipeline that produced the data might scan 200 GB a day. The consumption side is often the larger bill, and it is the side nobody instruments. When investigating cost, look at queries by source before you look at your own jobs. The cost nobody puts on the invoice Engineer time is usually the largest line item in a data platform, and it appears in no cloud console. An architecture that saves $2,000 a month in compute but needs a full-time person to keep running is not cheaper — it is roughly five times more expensive, and the difference is invisible in the tooling you would normally use to evaluate it. This is the real argument against premature complexity. A lakehouse with hand-rolled compaction, a streaming path nobody fully understands, and four tools where one would do — each of those has a monthly cost measured in attention. When you compare designs, put an engineer-hours row in the table. Governance: design inputs, not paperwork Privacy, access and deletion are usually presented as compliance obligations. Treat them instead as architectural constraints that are cheap on day one and expensive on day four hundred. Know where the personal data is Tag columns as they are created. You cannot protect what you have not inventoried, and reconstructing an inventory across 400 tables afterwards is a project rather than a task. Minimise The cheapest way to secure a field is to not have it. Do not ingest personal data you have no use for — and \"we might want it later\" is not a use. Mask or tokenise early Most analytics needs to group by a person, not identify them. If you hash or tokenise the identifier at ingestion, you can still count customers, measure retention and build cohorts — without holding the email address in a table twelve people can query. Where the real value is needed, use column-level masking and role-based access rather than a separate copy of the table, which immediately becomes a second thing to secure. Access control Grant to roles, not people, and on schemas rather than individual tables where you can. Per-person, per-table grants become unmaintainable within a year, and the failure mode is that nobody dares revoke anything. Deletion and retention Every table needs a defined end of life. The final phase is the one teams skip, which is why storage bills only ever grow. Immutable append-only architectures and the right to erasure GDPR and similar regimes give individuals the right to have their data deleted. If your architecture is built on immutable files that are only ever appended to, honouring that request is genuinely difficult — the data is spread across thousands of files, in backups, and in every downstream table derived from them. Design for it early. Keep personal data in a small, known set of places rather than copied through every layer. Choose a table format that supports row-level deletes. And keep a mapping that lets you find every row belonging to one person without scanning the entire lake. This is the clearest example in the course of a requirement that is nearly free to design for and very expensive to retrofit. Audit Log who queried what. You will eventually need it — either because of a compliance question, or because you need to know who is still using the table you want to delete. Putting a number on a design When you propose an architecture, estimate its cost to an order of magnitude. You do not need precision; you need to know whether you are proposing something that costs $200 a month or $20,000. A rough method that is good enough for a design discussion: Storage — total data volume × $25/TB/month. Usually a small number, and usually not the problem. Pipeline compute — data scanned per run × runs per day × your platform's rate. Consumption compute — estimated queries per day × average scan. This is often larger than the pipeline; do not skip it. Engineer time — how many hours a month does this design demand from a person? Multiply by a loaded hourly rate. Then name the biggest driver and say what you would do if the bill doubled. Being able to answer that question is a strong signal in a design review, and it is a question people are rarely prepared for. Checklist Explain why compute, not storage, dominates cloud data costs Name four common cost blowups and the fix for each Explain why the consumption side is often more expensive than the pipeline, and how you would check Argue against a technically cheaper architecture on engineer-time grounds Say why an immutable append-only design makes the right to erasure hard, and what you would do differently Produce an order-of-magnitude cost estimate for a design, and name its biggest driver"},{"slug":"framework","title":"The framework","lede":"A repeatable method for turning a vague request into a defensible design. The order matters — most bad designs come from choosing tools at step one instead of step five.","headings":[{"id":"the-mistake-this-framework-prevents","text":"The mistake this framework prevents"},{"id":"step-1-who-uses-this-and-what-do-they-decide","text":"Step 1 — Who uses this, and what do they decide?"},{"id":"step-2-get-numbers-never-adjectives","text":"Step 2 — Get numbers, never adjectives"},{"id":"step-3-sketch-the-flow-before-naming-anything","text":"Step 3 — Sketch the flow before naming anything"},{"id":"step-4-choose-ingestion-per-source","text":"Step 4 — Choose ingestion, per source"},{"id":"step-5-choose-storage-format-and-layout","text":"Step 5 — Choose storage, format and layout"},{"id":"step-6-choose-processing-and-schedule","text":"Step 6 — Choose processing and schedule"},{"id":"step-7-break-it-on-purpose","text":"Step 7 — Break it on purpose"},{"id":"step-8-say-how-it-is-operated","text":"Step 8 — Say how it is operated"},{"id":"applying-it-when-the-requirements-are-vague","text":"Applying it when the requirements are vague"},{"id":"checklist","text":"Checklist"}],"text":"The mistake this framework prevents Asked to design a data platform, the untrained instinct is to start naming things. \"We'd use Kafka, land it in S3, Spark for processing, Snowflake for serving, Airflow to orchestrate.\" It sounds fluent. It is the wrong first move, and an experienced interviewer will hear it as memorised rather than reasoned. The problem is that no one has yet said how much data there is, how fresh it must be, or who is going to use it. Every one of those tool choices might be right. None of them is justified , and a design you cannot justify is one you cannot defend when a constraint changes. Tools are chosen at steps four to six, and only after steps one and two have given you something to justify them against. Step 1 — Who uses this, and what do they decide? Name the person and the decision. \"A dashboard for the operations team to set tomorrow's staffing\" tells you far more than \"a reporting pipeline\" — it tells you the freshness requirement (daily, before the shift is planned), the consumers (a handful of people, not an application), and the correctness bar (approximately right is fine). If you cannot name the decision, you cannot judge any of the trade-offs that follow. Ask. Step 2 — Get numbers, never adjectives This is the step that most distinguishes an experienced designer, and the one beginners skip. \"Large volume\" and \"near real-time\" are not requirements; they are feelings. Ask about The question What it decides Volume Rows per day, bytes per day, total history? Single-node versus distributed. Often the answer is \"one machine is fine\" Growth Twice this in a year, or a hundred times? Whether today's design survives, and how much to over-build Freshness Daily, hourly, seconds? Batch versus streaming — the single biggest cost lever Query pattern Ad-hoc SQL, fixed dashboards, or lookups by key? The serving layer, and whether a warehouse is even appropriate Correctness Approximate, or reconcile to the penny? How much reliability machinery is justified Mutability Can records change or be deleted after creation? The ingestion pattern — see Ingestion Access Who may see personal data? Masking, layout, which layer holds what Budget Dollars per month, and how many engineers? Everything. And it is the constraint people forget to state If you take one habit into an interview Take this one. Ask for numbers before proposing anything. It is the clearest signal of experience available to you, it costs ninety seconds, and it makes every subsequent statement defensible. \"I'd use a single-node DuckDB job here, because 40 GB a day with daily freshness does not justify a cluster\" is a strong answer. \"I'd use Spark\" is not, even when Spark turns out to be right. Step 3 — Sketch the flow before naming anything Draw the five boxes from Foundations and fill them in at the level of \"an event stream lands here, gets cleaned here, is served from here\". Get agreement on the shape before debating any single component. This matters because arguments about tools are usually arguments about shape in disguise. Two people disagreeing about Kafka versus a nightly export are really disagreeing about whether the pipeline is event-driven — and settling the shape first makes the tool question answer itself. Step 4 — Choose ingestion, per source For each source separately: snapshot, high-water-mark, CDC, events, or file drop? Justify with the numbers from step 2 — volume, mutability, whether deletes matter, and freshness. Different sources in the same system will use different patterns. A design that applies one pattern to everything is usually a design that has not looked at the sources individually. Step 5 — Choose storage, format and layout Warehouse or lakehouse? Which file format? What is the partition key, and does it match the dominant filter in step 1's query pattern? What is the retention policy? State the partition key explicitly. It is a small decision that carries a large share of the eventual cost, and naming it shows you know that. Step 6 — Choose processing and schedule Batch or streaming. Full refresh or incremental. What the DAG looks like and what the schedule is. Then say, explicitly, how idempotency is achieved — overwrite by partition, merge on a key, or dedup on read. Volunteering this is one of the strongest signals available in a design conversation, because it shows you are thinking about the second run rather than the first. Step 7 — Break it on purpose This is the step that separates a strong design from a merely plausible one. Walk through the failure modes out loud, before anyone asks: The job fails halfway. What state is left? Is a retry safe? The source is down for twelve hours. Do we alert, wait, or publish stale data — and who decides? Data arrives three days late. Does it get counted? Where does it land? The source adds or renames a column. What breaks, and how do we find out? Someone reruns last month by accident. What is the blast radius? Volume goes up tenfold overnight. What breaks first? A bug shipped two weeks ago. How do we fix history, and what do we tell people who used the old numbers? You will not have a perfect answer to all seven. That is fine — \"we would accept publishing stale data with a banner, and here is why\" is a legitimate answer. Having considered them is the point. Step 8 — Say how it is operated Monitoring, tests, alerts, ownership, runbook, and an order-of-magnitude cost estimate. Name the biggest cost driver and what you would cut first if the bill doubled. Designs are often presented as though they will be built and then exist. They will be operated for years by people who did not build them, and a design that does not describe its own operation is incomplete. Applying it when the requirements are vague You will often be handed \"we want a data platform\". Do not design one. Find one consumer with one concrete question. Build the thinnest end-to-end path that answers it: one source, raw, one mart, one dashboard. Put orchestration, tests and monitoring around that thin path from day one — those are what make it a system rather than a script. Add the second source. Then the third. Generalise only when you have seen the same problem three times. Why the thin slice wins Platforms designed up front for imagined future needs are consistently worse than platforms that grew from one working pipeline — because the imagined needs are wrong in ways you cannot discover without a real consumer, and every wrong guess becomes a constraint you then design around. The vertical slice is the correct first move almost every time, and saying so in an interview is a better answer than a comprehensive architecture diagram. Checklist Recite the eight steps in order, and explain why tool selection sits at step four rather than step one List the eight things you would ask for numbers on before proposing a design Run the seven failure-mode questions against a system you already know Explain what you would build first when handed a vague \"we want a data platform\" brief Volunteer, unprompted, how idempotency is achieved in a design you are describing"},{"slug":"examples","title":"Worked examples","lede":"Two designs carried through the framework end to end, including the trade-offs that were accepted rather than solved. The reasoning is the point, not the architecture.","headings":[{"id":"example-1-an-analytics-warehouse-for-an-e-commerce-company","text":"Example 1 — An analytics warehouse for an e-commerce company"},{"id":"step-2-the-numbers","text":"Step 2: the numbers"},{"id":"the-decisions-and-why","text":"The decisions, and why"},{"id":"example-2-clickstream-events-with-near-real-time-product-ana","text":"Example 2 — Clickstream events with near-real-time product analytics"},{"id":"step-2-the-numbers","text":"Step 2: the numbers"},{"id":"the-decisions-and-why","text":"The decisions, and why"},{"id":"what-both-examples-have-in-common","text":"What both examples have in common"},{"id":"checklist","text":"Checklist"}],"text":"Example 1 — An analytics warehouse for an e-commerce company Step 2: the numbers Sources Postgres app database (50 M orders, ~2 M rows updated daily), Stripe API, Google Ads API Consumers 12 analysts writing SQL; about 30 dashboards Freshness Hourly is plenty. Daily acceptable for ad spend History 5 years Correctness Revenue must reconcile with Stripe to the cent Mutability Orders are updated (status changes) and cancelled orders are hard-deleted Access Emails and addresses restricted to two people Budget Modest. Team of two data engineers Three sources, three different ingestion patterns — because the right pattern is a property of the source, not of the platform. The decisions, and why A cloud warehouse, not a lakehouse. The data is in the low terabytes, the team is two SQL-fluent people, and there is no requirement for multiple query engines or open formats. A lakehouse would add compaction, table maintenance and tuning work to a team that has no spare capacity for it. Complexity would buy nothing here. CDC for Postgres. This is forced by the requirements, not chosen by preference. Orders get updated as their status changes, and cancelled orders are hard-deleted. A high-water-mark on updated_at would keep deleted orders forever — silently inflating order counts with no error anywhere — and would also miss the bulk migration that touched 400,000 rows without updating timestamps. CDC sees both. Full snapshot for Google Ads. The table is small, and the vendor restates the last three days of attribution data anyway. A snapshot self-corrects for free; incremental logic here would be more code doing a worse job. Incremental with a three-day lookback for Stripe. Refunds and disputes attach to charges from weeks ago, so a strict watermark would miss them. Reprocessing three days each run and upserting on charge_id is cheap insurance — a few minutes of compute against a class of silent revenue errors. Layers. raw (untouched, never edited, five years kept) → staging (typed, deduplicated, one row per entity) → marts (a star schema: fct_order_items , dim_customer , dim_product , dim_date ). Grain, declared before any SQL: one row per order line item . Analysts need revenue per product, so the order-level grain would not support the questions being asked. Writing this down first prevents the mixed-grain disaster described in Modelling . SCD Type 2 on dim_customer , for two fields only — country and segment . Historical reporting must attribute an old order to the segment the customer was in at the time. Everything else on that dimension is Type 1, because versioning fields nobody asks historical questions about is pure cost. PII. Emails and addresses stay in a restricted schema that two people can query. dim_customer in marts carries a hashed identifier and coarse geography — enough to group and cohort, not enough to identify. Idempotency. Marts are insert-overwrite by order_date partition. The Stripe load merges on charge_id . Both are safe to rerun, which makes the three-day lookback and any future backfill safe by construction. Tests. Uniqueness on every mart primary key, not-null on foreign keys, freshness on fct_order_items — and a daily reconciliation comparing warehouse revenue against Stripe's own reported total. That last one is the test that catches errors nothing else would. The trade-offs accepted, not solved CDC adds a moving part and requires replication access plus cooperation from whoever owns the database. Accepted, because the alternative silently loses deleted orders. Hourly refresh means dashboards can be up to sixty minutes stale. Accepted explicitly by the consumers, in writing, rather than assumed. The three-day Stripe lookback costs a little compute every day to prevent an error class that might occur monthly. Accepted, because a silent revenue error is far more expensive than the compute. Naming what you accepted is as important as naming what you chose. A design with no acknowledged trade-offs has not been examined. Example 2 — Clickstream events with near-real-time product analytics Step 2: the numbers Volume 50 M events/day — about 600/sec average, 4,000/sec peak. ~1.5 KB each Consumers Product analytics team, plus a live dashboard for a launch Freshness 5 minutes for the live dashboard; daily for behavioural aggregates Lateness Mobile clients go offline. Measured p99.9 arrival delay: about 4 days Correctness Approximate is fine for the live view. The daily aggregate must be right Two paths from one stream, serving two different promises. The split is deliberate, because one pipeline cannot be both fast and complete. The decisions, and why A dual path, deliberately. The live dashboard tolerates approximation; the daily aggregate does not. Rather than forcing one pipeline to satisfy both — which would mean either slow live numbers or wrong daily ones — the stream serves speed and the batch serves truth. Is this not Lambda, which you said was flawed? It is Lambda-shaped, and the flaw does not apply here. Lambda's problem is two implementations of the same business logic , which drift apart until the two answers disagree and nobody can say which is right. These two paths compute genuinely different things: the stream produces live counters, the batch produces sessions and funnels. There is no shared logic to drift, so the criticism does not bite. The test to apply: if both paths implement the same definition, you have Lambda's problem. If they answer different questions, you have two pipelines that happen to share a source. Kafka partitioned by user_id , so one user's events stay in order — which sessionisation requires. Seven-day retention sets the replay budget, and therefore the deadline on noticing a bug. Land raw to object storage first , unmodified, from the same topic. When a client-side tracking bug is found — and with mobile clients, one will be — all history can be reprocessed. A seven-day lookback on the batch path , chosen from the measured p99.9 of four days rather than picked from the air. Numbers for the last week are therefore expected to move slightly, which is documented for consumers rather than hidden. Both timestamps stored. event_time from the client — untrusted, clock-skewed, and clamped to a sane range before use — and ingested_at from the server, which is trusted. Aggregates use event time; operational monitoring uses ingestion time. Idempotency. Clients generate an event_id UUID at the moment the event occurs, not at ingestion. The batch deduplicates on it with ROW_NUMBER() , because at-least-once delivery and client-side retries both produce duplicates. Compaction. The streaming sink writes every five minutes, which would produce roughly 105,000 files a year per topic. A nightly job rewrites each day's partition into ~256 MB files. This is scheduled work, budgeted for, not something to discover later. Serving. Live counters go to Redis, not the warehouse. A dashboard polling every ten seconds against a warehouse would be both slow and startlingly expensive — see the dashboard cost trap in Cost and governance . The trade-offs accepted Two code paths mean two things to operate, and one more thing for an on-call engineer to understand. Accepted, because the alternative fails one of the two requirements. Live and daily numbers will not match exactly. Accepted — and handled by labelling the live panel as approximate. Managing that expectation is part of the design, not an afterthought. What both examples have in common The ingestion pattern was derived from the source's mutability , not chosen by preference. Idempotency was stated explicitly for every write, rather than assumed. The lateness policy came from a measured distribution, not an intuition. Raw data is landed unmodified in both, for the same reason: future bugs. The trade-offs are named and accepted, not hidden. That list is roughly what an interviewer is listening for, and roughly what a colleague reviewing your design will check. Checklist Explain why example 1 uses three different ingestion patterns for three sources Say why CDC is forced rather than preferred in example 1, referring to a specific requirement Explain when a Lambda-shaped architecture is acceptable and when it is not Justify a lookback window using a lateness distribution Name three trade-offs each design accepted rather than solved"},{"slug":"path","title":"Practice path","lede":"Reading about this field has sharply diminishing returns. This is the build order that turns the previous lessons into something you can actually do.","headings":[{"id":"how-to-use-this","text":"How to use this"},{"id":"weeks-12-sql-properly","text":"Weeks 1–2 · SQL, properly"},{"id":"weeks-34-python-for-moving-data","text":"Weeks 3–4 · Python for moving data"},{"id":"weeks-56-storage-formats-and-modelling","text":"Weeks 5–6 · Storage, formats and modelling"},{"id":"weeks-78-the-transformation-layer","text":"Weeks 7–8 · The transformation layer"},{"id":"weeks-910-orchestration","text":"Weeks 9–10 · Orchestration"},{"id":"weeks-1112-scale-and-streaming","text":"Weeks 11–12 · Scale and streaming"},{"id":"after-the-twelve-weeks","text":"After the twelve weeks"},{"id":"checklist","text":"Checklist"}],"text":"How to use this Every block ends with something that runs on your own machine. The weeks are a suggestion; the ordering is not, because each one depends on the last. Each block produces a working artefact, and each one is a prerequisite for the next. The order is dependency, not importance. Weeks 1–2 · SQL, properly Not SELECT ... WHERE . The things that data engineering actually runs on: window functions, CTEs, self-joins, GROUP BY with HAVING , date arithmetic, and reading a query plan. Window functions especially. Deduplication, sessionisation, running totals, and slowly changing dimensions are all written with them, and every one of those appears in the lessons above. Project Load a public dataset over 1 GB — NYC taxi trips is the classic — into DuckDB on your laptop and answer ten non-trivial questions with SQL. DuckDB is the fastest way to experience columnar analytics with no cloud account, no cluster, and no setup. Install it and you are running an OLAP engine in about thirty seconds. Weeks 3–4 · Python for moving data Requests and pagination, retries with exponential backoff, reading and writing Parquet with pyarrow or polars , handling secrets, and structured logging. Project Write an extractor for a free public API that lands raw JSON to disk, partitioned by date, then converts it to Parquet. Then make it safe to rerun — same input, same output, no duplicates — and prove it by running it three times and comparing row counts. That proof is the point of the exercise. It is Reliability made concrete. Weeks 5–6 · Storage, formats and modelling Re-read Storage and Modelling , then measure the claims yourself. Compare file sizes and query times for the same data as CSV against Parquet, partitioned against not. Watching a 30-second query become 0.4 seconds on your own machine teaches this better than any explanation. Project Model your dataset into a star schema. Write the grain of your fact table in a comment — \"one row per…\" — before writing any SQL. Then implement one SCD Type 2 dimension with valid_from , valid_to and a surrogate key. Weeks 7–8 · The transformation layer Learn dbt, or SQLMesh. Sources, models, ref() , materialisations, tests and documentation. Build a raw → staging → marts project with real layering. Project Convert weeks 5–6's SQL into a dbt project with at least a uniqueness test, a not-null test, a relationship test, and one incremental model. Then break something upstream on purpose — rename a column, duplicate a key, null out a field — and watch which test catches it and which does not. The tests that fail to fire are the interesting result. Weeks 9–10 · Orchestration Airflow, Dagster or Prefect. Focus on DAG structure, the logical-date parameter, retries, sensors and backfills rather than on the tool's configuration surface. Project — the one that teaches the most Schedule your pipeline to run daily. Let it run for several days. Then delete a week of output and backfill it . If the result afterwards is byte-identical to before, your pipeline is idempotent and correctly parameterised. If you get duplicates, or seven copies of today's data, you have just reproduced the single most common production bug in data engineering — on your laptop, where it costs nothing. Weeks 11–12 · Scale and streaming Spark fundamentals — partitions, shuffle, skew, spill — enough to read a job and reason about why it is slow. Then Kafka basics: producing, consuming, offsets, partitions. Project Run Kafka locally. Produce synthetic events, consume them into partitioned Parquet, and compute a windowed aggregate. Then kill the consumer mid-run and restart it. Count the rows before and after. Whatever you observe — duplicates, gaps, or neither — is Reliability happening to you in person, and it will stay learned. After the twelve weeks Read Designing Data-Intensive Applications properly , a chapter a week. It will be much easier now that you have built something, and much more useful than it would have been at week one. Retrofit the hard parts. Take a pipeline you already built and add the things from Quality and Cost : freshness monitoring, the six test categories, a runbook, a cost measurement. Practise the framework out loud. How would you design analytics for a music streaming service? A food delivery app? A hospital? Say the answer aloud using the eight steps . Fluency in the framework is a separate skill from knowing the content, and it only comes from rehearsal. A note on portfolios A small pipeline that is idempotent, tested, monitored, documented, and honest about its trade-offs is worth more than an elaborate one that only works when nothing goes wrong. Interviewers can tell the difference within about two minutes, and the second kind is much more common — which means the first kind stands out more than its size would suggest. A README that says \"here is what this does, here is what breaks it, here is what I would do differently at ten times the volume\" is a stronger artefact than another five services. Checklist Have a running query engine on your own machine and a dataset over a gigabyte in it Have written an extractor you have proven is safe to rerun Have measured the CSV-to-Parquet difference yourself rather than taking this course's word for it Have watched a data test catch a bug you introduced deliberately Have performed a backfill and verified the output was unchanged Have killed a streaming consumer mid-run and looked at what happened to the data"},{"slug":"reference","title":"Glossary & numbers","lede":"The lookup page. Terms you will hear in meetings, numbers worth carrying in your head, and the four books that matter.","headings":[{"id":"numbers-worth-memorising","text":"Numbers worth memorising"},{"id":"ten-principles-condensed","text":"Ten principles, condensed"},{"id":"glossary","text":"Glossary"},{"id":"four-books-in-reading-order","text":"Four books, in reading order"},{"id":"things-to-practise-on","text":"Things to practise on"}],"text":"Numbers worth memorising Having a figure to hand is what lets you say \"that will not work\" in a design meeting rather than \"hmm, maybe\". Rule Value Target Parquet file size 128 MB – 1 GB Target partition size 100 MB – 1 GB Typical CSV → Parquet size reduction 5–10× Typical columnar compression on a single column 5–20× Object storage cost ~$20–25 per TB per month Relative cost of streaming versus batch engineering Roughly 5–10× the effort \"Just use a warehouse\" threshold Comfortably under ~10 TB \"One machine is fine\" threshold Far higher than beginners assume — DuckDB and Polars handle hundreds of GB on a laptop Default partition key Date Default freshness Daily. Justify hourly. Prove the need for streaming Times a source column will change without warning More than zero Ten principles, condensed Keep raw data forever and never edit it. Make every write idempotent. Parameterise by logical date, never CURRENT_DATE . Partition by what you filter on. Prefer batch until a named decision forces otherwise. Test freshness, volume and uniqueness on everything that matters. Define business logic exactly once. Declare the grain before writing the query. Store both event time and ingestion time. Choose the simplest thing that meets the stated numeric requirement — then write down the trade-off you made. Glossary Term Meaning ACID Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability — transaction guarantees. Table formats bring them to object storage Backfill Rerunning a pipeline over a past date range to repopulate history CDC Change Data Capture — reading a database's replication log to stream every insert, update and delete Clustering Physically ordering rows within files so file-level statistics become selective. Also called sorting or Z-ordering Compaction Rewriting many small files into fewer large ones Data contract An agreement between producer and consumer on schema, semantics, freshness and how changes happen DAG Directed Acyclic Graph — how orchestrators model task dependencies Denormalisation Deliberately duplicating data to avoid joins. Standard practice in analytics Dimension A table of descriptive context — who, what, where ELT / ETL Load-then-transform versus transform-then-load Event time When something actually happened, as opposed to when you received it Fact A table of things that happened, holding measures and foreign keys Grain What exactly one row of a table represents High-water mark The largest timestamp or ID seen so far, used to pull only newer rows Idempotent Running it twice gives the same result as running it once Lakehouse Warehouse-like semantics — ACID, schema, time travel — over open files in object storage Lineage The dependency graph of which datasets feed which Logical date The period a pipeline run is processing, passed in as a parameter Materialisation How a model is persisted: view, table, or incremental OLAP / OLTP Analytical versus transactional workloads Partition A physical subdivision of a table, usually by date. Also a unit of parallel work in distributed engines Predicate pushdown Pushing filters into the storage layer so unneeded data is never read Restatement Historical numbers changing because late or corrected data arrived SCD Slowly Changing Dimension. Type 1 overwrites, Type 2 versions Schema evolution Changing a schema over time without breaking readers Shuffle Network movement of data between nodes. Usually the expensive step Skew Uneven data distribution causing one task to dominate runtime Star schema One fact table joined to surrounding dimensions Surrogate key A generated key identifying one version of a record, as opposed to the business key identifying the thing itself Table format Iceberg, Delta, Hudi — the metadata layer that turns files into a table Upsert / MERGE Insert if new, update if existing, keyed on a business key Watermark An engine's estimate of how complete event-time data is, which lets windows close Four books, in reading order Read first Fundamentals of Data Engineering Reis & Housley. The best single overview of the field, and the closest thing to an expanded version of this course. Start here. Reference The Data Warehouse Toolkit Kimball & Ross. The canonical text on dimensional modelling. Dense — read the first four chapters properly, then treat it as a lookup. After you build Designing Data-Intensive Applications Kleppmann. The deep foundations: replication, partitioning, consistency, consensus, stream processing. Harder, and worth it — but read it once you have shipped something. If you go deep Streaming Systems Akidau, Chernyak & Lax. The clearest treatment of event time and watermarks anywhere. Only when streaming becomes your problem. Things to practise on DuckDB — the best local sandbox for columnar analytics. No account, no cluster, no setup. Public datasets — NYC TLC trip records, GH Archive, Wikipedia pageviews, or any large Kaggle set. Documentation worth reading directly — dbt's guides on incremental models and testing; the Iceberg or Delta specification overview; Kafka's design documentation. Primary sources are consistently better than blog summaries in this field, because the summaries all copy each other."},{"slug":"quiz","title":"Practice quiz","lede":"Twenty-six questions, filterable by topic. Read the explanation even when you got it right — most of them name the tempting wrong answer and why it is wrong, which is the part that transfers.","headings":[{"id":"choose-a-category","text":"Choose a category"}],"text":"Choose a category All Foundations Building blocks The hard parts Designing systems"}];
